          <a href={base + '/flows'}>&larr; Grant Types</a>
          <a href={base + '/tokens'}>Tokens &amp; JWT &rarr;</a>
        </nav>
      </nav>

      <main class="content">
        <div class="page-header">
          <span class="badge badge-intermediate">Intermediate &middot; 35 min</span>
          <h1>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</h1>
          <p class="lead">Understanding the modern identity layer built on OAuth 2.0, the history of federated identity, solving the pseudo-authentication problem, ID Token verification, dynamic discovery, and internet-scale BYOI federation.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- EVOLUTION -->
        <section id="evolution">
          <h2>The Evolution of Identity Protocols</h2>
          <p>The web's approach to identity and authentication progressed through 4 distinct eras:</p>

          <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap:1rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--danger); margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">1. Proprietary Silos</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>1990s &ndash; Early 2000s</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Every app required its own username/password database or proprietary vendor LDAP connectors. Zero interoperability.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:#fbbf24; margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">2. SAML 2.0 (XML)</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>2005 &ndash; Enterprise Standard</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Heavy XML assertions and manual certificate federation. Reliable for enterprise web SSO, but brittle for mobile apps and SPAs.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">3. OAuth 2.0 (RFC 6749)</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>2012 &ndash; REST/JSON AuthZ</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Lightweight delegated authorization using HTTP and JSON. Left token formats and authentication semantics undefined.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid #34d399; padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:#34d399; margin-top:0; font-size:1rem;">4. OpenID Connect (OIDC)</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.85rem; color:var(--muted);"><strong>2014+ &ndash; Modern Standard</strong></p>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Combines OAuth 2.0 transport with signed JWT ID Tokens, standardized scopes, discovery, and dynamic federation.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- PSEUDO AUTH -->
        <section id="pseudo-auth">
          <h2>The Pseudo-Authentication Problem</h2>
          <p>Before OpenID Connect, developers attempted to use raw OAuth 2.0 Access Tokens as proof of user identity. As documented by oauth.net and Okta's identity architecture team:</p>
          
          <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid #fbbf24; border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="font-weight:700; color:#fbbf24; margin-bottom:0.75rem; font-size:1.05rem;">🎫 The Movie Ticket vs. Passport Analogy</div>
            <p style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>"Using an Access Token as proof of identity is like using a movie ticket as a passport:"</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Movie Ticket (Access Token):</strong> Proves someone paid for Seat 14B in Theater 3. The usher does not care <em>who</em> you are, only that you have a valid ticket to enter the room.</li>
              <li><strong>Passport (ID Token):</strong> An official, tamper-proof credential issued by an authority stating your full legal name, date of birth, photo, and citizenship.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <h3>Why Raw OAuth Fails for Authentication:</h3>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Vulnerability / Gap</th><th>Technical Cause</th><th>Security Consequence</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Audience Mismatch</strong></td>
                <td>Access token audience (<code>aud</code>) is the backend Resource Server, not the Client application.</td>
                <td><strong>Token Replay Attack:</strong> A malicious App A can take an Access Token issued to it and replay it to log into App B.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Opaque Format</strong></td>
                <td>OAuth 2.0 intentionally left access token formats undefined (often random hexadecimal strings).</td>
                <td>Client cannot safely inspect user claims, timestamps, or issuer signatures locally without an extra server call.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>No Standard User Info Schema</strong></td>
                <td>Every provider returned profile data in incompatible JSON shapes (e.g., Google vs GitHub vs Facebook).</td>
                <td>Developers had to write custom parsers and maintain brittle API adapters for every identity provider.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- WHAT IS -->
        <section id="what-is">
          <h2>What is OpenID Connect (OIDC Core 1.0)?</h2>
          <p>OpenID Connect combined the best features of <strong>OAuth 2.0</strong>, <strong>Facebook Connect</strong>, and <strong>SAML 2.0</strong> to establish a standardized, lightweight identity layer:</p>

          <div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap:1rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">1. The ID Token</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">A cryptographically signed JWT intended exclusively for the Client application to prove the user authenticated.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">2. UserInfo Endpoint</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">Standardized REST endpoint (<code>GET /userinfo</code>) returning validated, normalized JSON user profile claims.</p>
            </div>
            <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); padding:1.25rem; border-radius:var(--radius);">
              <h3 style="color:var(--primary); margin-top:0;">3. Dynamic Discovery</h3>
              <p style="font-size:0.88rem; margin-bottom:0;">JSON metadata at <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code> eliminating manual federation configuration.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SAML VS OIDC -->
        <section id="saml-vs-oidc">
          <h2>SAML 2.0 vs OIDC vs OAuth 2.0</h2>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>SAML 2.0</th><th>OAuth 2.0</th><th>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Protocol Purpose</strong></td><td>Enterprise SSO &amp; Federation</td><td>Delegated Authorization</td><td>Modern Identity &amp; SSO</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Token Format</strong></td><td>Signed XML Assertion</td><td>Opaque string or JWT</td><td>JSON Web Token (ID Token)</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Transport Layer</strong></td><td>HTTP POST XML Binding</td><td>HTTPS REST / JSON</td><td>HTTPS REST / JSON</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Metadata Discovery</strong></td><td>Manual XML file download</td><td>None (Vendor defined)</td><td>Dynamic Discovery JSON</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Mobile &amp; SPA Support</strong></td><td>Poor (Heavy XML payloads)</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Excellent</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Assurance Levels</strong></td><td>AuthnContextClassRef</td><td>None</td><td><code>acr</code> and <code>amr</code> claims</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- ACTORS -->
        <section id="actors">
          <h2>OIDC Actors (Standard Terminology)</h2>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>End-User (Resource Owner):</strong> The human whose identity is being verified.</li>
            <li><strong>Relying Party (RP / Client):</strong> The application requesting identity proof from the OpenID Provider.</li>
            <li><strong>OpenID Provider (OP / Authorization Server):</strong> The identity server (Okta, Entra ID, Google) that authenticates the user and issues ID Tokens.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- ID TOKEN -->
        <section id="id-token">
          <h2>The ID Token Anatomy (RFC 7519 &amp; OIDC Core 1.0)</h2>
          <p>The ID Token is a signed JSON Web Token containing standardized identity assertions:</p>

          <pre><code><span class="c">// Decoded ID Token Payload (JSON)</span>
&#123;
  "iss": "https://authorization-server.example.com",
  "sub": "usr_998877",
  "aud": "my_client_app_id",
  "exp": 1755003600,
  "iat": 1755000000,
  "auth_time": 1754999990,
  "nonce": "n-0S6_WzA2Mj",
  "at_hash": "T7ij7o69gBtjo6bAJvaVBQ",
  "amr": ["pwd", "mfa"],
  "idp": "00o1zyyqo9bpRehCw1t7",
  "name": "Alice Chen",
  "email": "alice@authmatrix.local",
  "email_verified": true,
  "roles": ["Admin"]
&#125;</code></pre>

          <div class="callout danger">
            <strong>⛔ Critical Security Rule:</strong>
            <p><strong>The ID Token is intended solely for the Client Application.</strong> Never send the ID Token to backend API servers in the <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> header. Backend APIs should only receive Access Tokens!</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- AT HASH -->
        <section id="at-hash">
          <h2>Cryptographic Hash Bindings: <code>at_hash</code> &amp; <code>c_hash</code></h2>
          <p>When tokens are issued via front-channel redirects, OIDC uses mathematical hashes inside the ID Token to prevent token injection and substitution attacks:</p>

          <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--primary); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin-bottom:0.75rem; font-size:1.05rem;">🔐 How <code>at_hash</code> Protects Access Tokens</div>
            <ol style="margin-bottom:0; line-height:1.7;">
              <li>The Authorization Server computes the SHA-256 hash of the ASCII Access Token: <code>hash = SHA256(access_token)</code>.</li>
              <li>It takes the left-most half of the hash (128 bits for SHA-256) and Base64URL-encodes it.</li>
              <li>It embeds this string into the ID Token as the <code>at_hash</code> claim.</li>
              <li>When the client receives both the ID Token and Access Token in the browser, it hashes the Access Token and verifies it matches <code>at_hash</code>. This mathematically proves that the Access Token came from the exact same issuance as the signed ID Token.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SCOPES CLAIMS -->
        <section id="scopes-claims">
          <h2>The 14 Standard OIDC Profile Claims</h2>
          <p>Requesting <code>scope=profile</code> grants access to the full standardized profile claim schema defined in <strong>OIDC Core Section 5.1</strong>:</p>

          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Claim Name</th><th>JSON Type</th><th>Description &amp; Purpose</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>String</td><td>Full display name of the user (e.g. "Alice Chen").</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>given_name</code></td><td>String</td><td>First / given name (e.g. "Alice").</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>family_name</code></td><td>String</td><td>Last / surname / family name (e.g. "Chen").</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>middle_name</code></td><td>String</td><td>Middle name or initial.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>nickname</code></td><td>String</td><td>Casual display name or handle.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>preferred_username</code></td><td>String</td><td>Primary login handle / corporate username (e.g. <code>alice.chen@company.com</code>).</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>profile</code></td><td>String (URL)</td><td>URL of the user's online profile page.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>picture</code></td><td>String (URL)</td><td>URL of the user's avatar / profile photo.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>website</code></td><td>String (URL)</td><td>URL of the user's personal or company website.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>gender</code></td><td>String</td><td>Gender representation.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>birthdate</code></td><td>String</td><td>Birthday formatted as YYYY-MM-DD (ISO 8601).</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>zoneinfo</code></td><td>String</td><td>Timezone (e.g. <code>America/Los_Angeles</code>, <code>Europe/London</code>).</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>locale</code></td><td>String</td><td>Locale string (e.g. <code>en-US</code>, <code>fr-CA</code>).</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>updated_at</code></td><td>Number</td><td>Unix timestamp when the profile was last modified.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- CLAIM ROUTING -->
        <section id="claim-routing">
          <h2>Where Do Claims Go? (ID Token vs <code>/userinfo</code>)</h2>
          <p>OIDC balances token size with performance based on the <code>response_type</code> and <code>scope</code> requested:</p>

          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Flow / Response Type</th><th>What is returned in the ID Token?</th><th>How to get remaining Profile Claims?</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Identity Only</strong><br/>(<code>response_type=id_token</code>)</td>
                <td><strong>ALL requested profile claims</strong> (<code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>locale</code>, etc.) are embedded directly inside the ID Token JWT.</td>
                <td>Not applicable &mdash; no access token was issued to query <code>/userinfo</code>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Tokens + API Access</strong><br/>(<code>response_type=code</code> or <code>id_token token</code>)</td>
                <td><strong>Minimal claims only</strong> (<code>sub</code>, <code>iss</code>, <code>aud</code>, <code>exp</code>, <code>nonce</code>, <code>at_hash</code>) to keep token header sizes lightweight for HTTP headers.</td>
                <td>Call <code>GET /userinfo</code> using the Access Token: <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;access_token&gt;</code> to retrieve full profile claims.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <!-- DISCOVERY -->
        <section id="discovery">
          <h2>Dynamic Discovery &amp; BYOI (Bring Your Own Identity)</h2>
          <p>OIDC enables internet-scale dynamic federation without pre-sharing XML certificates. An application can discover everything about an IdP by querying <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>:</p>

          <pre><code>GET /.well-known/openid-configuration HTTP/1.1
Host: accounts.example.com

<span class="c">// Response</span>
&#123;
  "issuer": "https://accounts.example.com",
  "authorization_endpoint": "https://accounts.example.com/oauth2/v1/authorize",
  "token_endpoint": "https://accounts.example.com/oauth2/v1/token",
  "userinfo_endpoint": "https://accounts.example.com/oauth2/v1/userinfo",
  "jwks_uri": "https://accounts.example.com/oauth2/v1/keys",
  "response_types_supported": ["code", "id_token", "code id_token"],
  "subject_types_supported": ["public", "pairwise"],
  "id_token_signing_alg_values_supported": ["RS256"]
&#125;</code></pre>

          <div style="background:var(--surface); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:1.5rem; margin:1.5rem 0;">
            <div style="font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin-bottom:0.5rem; font-size:1.05rem;">🌐 How BYOI (Bring Your Own Identity) Works</div>
            <ol style="margin-bottom:0; line-height:1.7;">
              <li>User types their corporate email: <code>alice@acmecorp.com</code>.</li>
              <li>App extracts domain <code>acmecorp.com</code> and fetches <code>https://acmecorp.com/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>.</li>
              <li>App dynamically discovers Acme's authorization and JWKS endpoints.</li>
              <li>User logs in at Acme IdP and returns with an ID Token signed by Acme's public keys.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- EXECUTION FLOW -->
        <section id="execution-flow">
          <h2>5-Step OpenID Connect Flow</h2>

          <pre class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant RP as 💻 Relying Party (App)
    participant OP as 🌐 OpenID Provider (IdP)
    participant UserInfo as 👤 UserInfo Endpoint

    Note over RP,OP: Step 1: Dynamic Discovery (BYOI)
    RP-&gt;&gt;OP: 1. GET /.well-known/openid-configuration
    OP--&gt;&gt;RP: 2. Metadata (jwks_uri, auth_endpoint, token_endpoint)

    Note over RP,OP: Step 2: User Authentication &amp; Code Flow
    RP-&gt;&gt;OP: 3. GET /authorize (scope=openid profile email, nonce=n-0S6)
    OP--&gt;&gt;RP: 4. 302 Redirect with Authorization Code

    Note over RP,OP: Step 3: Token Exchange &amp; JWKS Key Fetch
    RP-&gt;&gt;OP: 5. POST /token (code, grant_type=authorization_code)
    OP--&gt;&gt;RP: 6. Tokens &#123; id_token (JWT), access_token &#125;
    RP-&gt;&gt;OP: 7. GET /.well-known/jwks.json
    OP--&gt;&gt;RP: 8. Public Verification Keys (kid, RSA Modulus, Exponent)

    Note over RP: Step 4: Local ID Token Verification<br/>Verify RS256 signature, iss, aud, exp, nonce

    Note over RP,UserInfo: Step 5: (Optional) Extended Profile Fetch
    RP-&gt;&gt;UserInfo: 9. GET /userinfo (Authorization: Bearer &lt;access_token&gt;)
    UserInfo--&gt;&gt;RP: 10. 200 OK &#123; sub, name, email, picture, roles &#125;
          </pre>
        </section>

        <!-- RESPONSE TYPES -->
        <section id="response-types">
          <h2>OIDC Response Types &amp; Hybrid Flow Matrix</h2>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th><code>response_type</code></th><th>Flow Category</th><th>Front-Channel Artifacts</th><th>Back-Channel Artifacts</th><th>Best Used For</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><code>code</code></td>
                <td>Authorization Code Flow</td>
                <td><code>code</code></td>
                <td><code>id_token</code>, <code>access_token</code>, <code>refresh_token</code></td>
                <td>Server-side web apps with middleware (Express, Spring Boot).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>id_token</code></td>
                <td>Implicit (Identity Only)</td>
                <td><code>id_token</code></td>
                <td>None</td>
                <td>Client app needing instant identity verification without API access.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>id_token token</code></td>
                <td>Implicit (Full)</td>
                <td><code>id_token</code>, <code>access_token</code></td>
                <td>None</td>
                <td>Legacy SPAs calling APIs directly (Deprecated in OAuth 2.1).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>code id_token</code></td>
                <td>Hybrid Flow</td>
                <td><code>code</code>, <code>id_token</code></td>
                <td><code>access_token</code>, <code>refresh_token</code></td>
                <td>Immediate client identity render + back-channel API session setup.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>code id_token token</code></td>
                <td>Hybrid Flow (Full)</td>
                <td><code>code</code>, <code>id_token</code>, <code>access_token</code></td>
                <td><code>refresh_token</code></td>
                <td>Client needs instant API access &amp; server needs refresh token.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </section>

        <footer class="page-footer">
          <div class="prev-next">
            <a href={base + '/flows'}>&larr; Grant Types</a>
            <a href={base + '/tokens'}>Next: Tokens &amp; JWT &rarr;</a>
          </div>
        </footer>
      </main>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
